 /*
- * Poco F1 (SDM845): run both CPU clusters at their top cpufreq-hw state.
- * XNU has no cpufreq driver, so the cores otherwise stay where ABL left
- * them, and everything (CoreAnimation renders on the CPU) runs slow.
- * SDM845 "qcom,cpufreq-hw" (Linux qcom-cpufreq-hw.c, v1 register layout,
- * as ios-topaz patch 0094 for SM6225): freq-domain0 (A55) 0x17d43000,
- * freq-domain1 (A75) 0x17d45800; LUT at +0x110 (32-byte rows, bits 31:30
- * clock source, 7:0 L-value x 19.2 MHz), PERF_STATE at +0x920.
- * The firmware (OSM/LMh) still applies its voltage and thermal limits.
- * Boot-arg sweetcpufreq=0 leaves the frequencies alone.
+ * Poco F1 (SDM845, beryllium): CPU frequency governor on the OSM /
+ * "qcom,cpufreq-hw" block. XNU has no cpufreq driver, so the clusters would
+ * stay wherever the firmware left them. Policy (user decision): the four
+ * Cortex-A75 (cluster 1, CPU 4-7) may go to the top LUT state under load, the
+ * four Cortex-A55 (cluster 0, CPU 0-3) are capped (default 50% of their max);
+ * both clusters fall to their lowest state when idle.
+ *
+ * Hardware facts (Linux drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c, "qcom,cpufreq-hw"
+ * v1 variant, qcom_soc_data{ reg_enable 0x0, reg_freq_lut 0x110, reg_volt_lut
+ * 0x114, reg_perf_state 0x920, lut_row_size 32 }; register facts only, no code
+ * taken from it):
+ *   ENABLE      +0x000  bit 0: domain enabled by the firmware
+ *   FREQ_LUT    +0x110 + 32 * i   bits 31:30 clock source (0 = the 300 MHz
+ *                                  alternate clock, GPLL0 / 2; else XO 19.2 MHz
+ *                                  x L-value), bits 7:0 L-value, bits 18:16
+ *                                  cores allowed at this state
+ *   VOLT_LUT    +0x114 + 32 * i   bits 11:0 voltage (Linux: mV)
+ *   PERF_STATE  +0x920  LUT index requested from / reported by the OSM
+ * The LUT ends where a row repeats the previous frequency.
+ * Live DT (dtb/beryllium/live.dts, /soc/qcom,cpufreq-hw): reg windows
+ * freq-domain0 0x17d43000 (A55, cpu@0-300) and freq-domain1 0x17d45800 (A75,
+ * cpu@400-700), 0x1400 bytes each, qcom,lut-max-entries = 27, clocks xo +
+ * alternate. The addresses are hard coded for this board (like the other
+ * sweet_* drivers); they do not need to pass through the ADT. The timer is the
+ * 19.2 MHz architectural counter (ADT timebase-frequency), independent of CPU
+ * frequency. The firmware (OSM / LMh) still applies its own voltage and
+ * thermal limits.
+ *
+ * Governor (default): a kernel thread samples every 25 ms. Load of a cluster =
+ * the busiest of its CPUs, 100% - idle residency, from the scheduler's
+ * per-processor idle time (recount, fed by processor_idle(), i.e. the WFI idle
+ * path). Rule: required = cur_khz * load / 80 (target utilisation 80%); load
+ * >= 90% jumps straight to the cluster's cap; the smallest LUT state with
+ * khz >= required (within [lowest, cap]) is the target. Up: immediately. Down:
+ * only after 3 consecutive samples (75 ms) below the current state, then by at
+ * most 2 LUT states per decision. At idle the target is the lowest state.
+ * A write is one MMIO store + dsb: no locks, safe from thread context.
+ *
+ * Boot-args:
+ *   sweetcpufreq=gov|fixed|off   (default gov; 0 = off, 1 = gov)
+ *     off   leave the frequencies as the firmware set them
+ *     fixed set each domain once (see _little / _big) and keep it
+ *   sweetcpufreq_little_max=<10..100>  gov: A55 cap, % of max (default 50)
+ *   sweetcpufreq_big_max=<10..100>     gov: A75 cap, % of max (default 100)
+ *   sweetcpufreq_little=<10..100>      fixed: A55 % of max (default 40)
+ *   sweetcpufreq_big=<10..100>         fixed: A75 % of max (default 100)
+ *   sweetcpufreq_log=<seconds>         time-in-state summary period (default
+ *                                      10, 0 = none)
+ *   sweetcpufreq_force=1               also act when ENABLE bit 0 is clear
+ * The closest LUT entry to the percentage is used (100 = highest index).
+ * Starts once every selected CPU is up (after iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetCPU.cpp's
+ * SMP bring-up). All logs start with "SWEET CPUFREQ:".
+ *
+ * sysctl kern.sweet_cpufreq (read-only string, stable format "v1", for the
+ * Bergamota metrics panel). Lines are separated by '\n'; each line is a
+ * sequence of space-separated key=value words:
+ *   line 1:  sweet_cpufreq v1 mode=<gov|fixed|off> period_ms=<sample period>
+ *   line 2:  cluster0 name=A55 cpus=0-3 state=<applied|off|skipped|pending>
+ *            mode=<gov|fixed|off> idx=<current LUT index> cur_khz=<current>
+ *            min_khz=<lowest LUT> max_khz=<highest LUT> cap_khz=<policy max,
+ *            gov cap or fixed target> lut_n=<entries> load=<last load %> changes=<perf
+ *            state writes> tis_ms=<khz>:<ms>,<khz>:<ms>,...  (every LUT state in
+ *            index order, cumulative milliseconds spent there since start; the
+ *            states are the ones listed in lut_khz=)
+ *   line 3:  cluster1 ... (A75, cpus=4-7), same keys
+ * idx/cur_khz are read live from PERF_STATE; they are 0 unless state=applied.
+ * lut_khz=<khz>,<khz>,... is also present on each cluster line (before tis_ms).
  */
